    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). There's a single test class (`AcceptanceTest`); the other files under `acceptance/` are the abstract base and a helper.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` \u2014 the number of existing owners sharing the same first and last name (case-insensitively) at creation time \u2014 persisted at create and returned on read.\n\nChanges, kept small and cohesive to limit structural impact:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a persisted `Integer namesakeCount` field with a trivial getter/setter (CC 1 each). No existing method touched.\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`** \u2014 one statement, reusing the already-fetched `owners` collection to count case-insensitive first+last-name matches *before* the save. No new controller method, so the large controller's surrounding complexity is paid only once.\n3. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `namesakeCount` as a read-only `int32` on the `Owner` read schema. MapStruct auto-maps it (matching property name), so `OwnerMapper` needed no change.\n4. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** (tests) and **`db/h2/schema.sql`** (app) \u2014 added the `namesake_count` column, following this repo's maintained-schema convention (mysql/postgres schemas are already stale \u2014 they lack `customer_code` too \u2014 so I left them out of scope).\n\nThe count is stored on the owner at creation, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` faithfully returns the \"before this create\" snapshot rather than a value that would drift as later namesakes are added.",
